te set-url origin \"$(git remote get-url origin | sed 's#^https\\?://[^/]*#'\"$HT_FORGEJO_URL\"'#')\" 2>/dev/null; git pull -q 2>&1|tail -2; git log --oneline|head -2; git diff 442d94e HEAD | grep '^[+-]' | cut -c1-1500"]], "output": "```console\n311f135 Outline rev 16: pooled C2 share is 1224 non-null cells, 85.1/86.7%\n442d94e Outline rev 15: frozen B2 kappa-hat at the grid floor read as a bound (Brief rev 13)\n--- a/Outline.md\n+++ b/Outline.md\n-**C2 — Bias is set by curvature × corner distance; independent of d for smooth responses in the fraction feature (with the mechanism qualifier below).** For a response curved in exposure, linear-fit spillover bias ≈ −½g''(1−2p) (+ third-order term); zero at p=½ to second order; for threshold responses with threshold outside the observed support the linear fit sees no spillover and the bias is the whole jump, independent of d in the limit of large d; at small d the support straddles the threshold and the bias is smaller or of opposite sign (`analysis` plan §3: the threshold slope does depend on d; the d-independence holds for smooth responses). Flexible learners do not remove it (trees flat, polynomials explode). The predictor used in the tests is the population-OLS limit on the realised graph and assignment law (closed-form traces for (a), (b), (e), a Binomial mixture for (c), (d), a closed form for (f)); \"exact\" is a statement about the form, not about agreement with the simulation, so the paper calls it **the population-OLS prediction** and reports the agreement separately (rev. 14, `idea-review`, `story-repo`#6); it is not the finite-sample bias and not the second-order expansion −½g''(1−2p), which the text uses to illustrate scalings only. Wording fixed by the Brief (rev. 7): \"independent of d\" was stated for a smooth response in the fraction feature and for a threshold beyond small d, and deferred count-type mechanisms to their own derivation. It holds \n+**C2 — Bias is set by curvature × corner distance; independent of d for smooth responses in the fraction feature (with the mechanism qualifier below).** For a response curved in exposure, linear-fit spillover bias ≈ −½g''(1−2p) (+ third-order term); zero at p=½ to second order; for threshold responses with threshold outside the observed support the linear fit sees no spillover and the bias is the whole jump, independent of d in the limit of large d; at small d the support straddles the threshold and the bias is smaller or of opposite sign (`analysis` plan §3: the threshold slope does depend on d; the d-independence holds for smooth responses). Flexible learners do not remove it (trees flat, polynomials explode). The predictor used in the tests is the population-OLS limit on the realised graph and assignment law (closed-form traces for (a), (b), (e), a Binomial mixture for (c), (d), a closed form for (f)); \"exact\" is a statement about the form, not about agreement with the simulation, so the paper calls it **the population-OLS prediction** and reports the agreement separately (rev. 14, `idea-review`, `story-repo`#6); it is not the finite-sample bias and not the second-order expansion −½g''(1−2p), which the text uses to illustrate scalings only. Wording fixed by the Brief (rev. 7): \"independent of d\" was stated for a smooth response in the fraction feature and for a threshold beyond small d, and deferred count-type mechanisms to their own derivation. It holds \n-| C2 bias = curvature × corner distance | supported where the account applies, **with d-dependence differing by mechanism** | exact population-OLS prediction (with the K=30 truncated operator the simulation uses and finite-n centring, `pred_bias_finite_n`) lies inside the MC interval in 86.6% of 1257 cells of (a)–(f) (85.1% without centring); (e) was 58% with the untruncated series, **81.4% with the truncated operator and 90.2% with centring (204 cells): the earlier gap was the truncation, so \"cause not diagnosed\" is dropped**; the lowest after (e) are (d) 81% and (b) 82%, not diagnosed | \"bias independent of d\" holds for (c),(e),(f); **not for (b)** (count fitted by fraction; the Brief deferred count mechanisms to their own derivation; 0.115→0.025→0.007 for d=5,20,80; checked by `analysis` (`c2_explain_d_b\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "3dd18f2a-22df-539f-9547-6b057ddde753", "at": 1795653437.9138682, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 110105, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "6504ec24dc5690e915e588cb1f273217", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "504fd54b-ad38-5f49-824c-fcc0c053b991", "at": 1795653437.9163342, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 110106, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "6504ec24dc5690e915e588cb1f273217", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "51a67a3c-7767-5517-9c00-7c8ea912680d", "at": 1795653437.91854, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 110107, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.response", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "6504ec24dc5690e915e588cb1f273217", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "response"}, {"id": "9894fa65-0b7b-5199-907a-25bd2232e5c3", "at": 1795653437.978386, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 110108, "layer": "worker", "category": "challenge", "kind": "authentication.challenge", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "unknown", "execution_id": "6504ec24dc5690e915e588cb1f273217", "trust": "system-confirm